--TEST--
An undeclared property is `E0405` on a reserved exception class exactly as on a user class, read and write alike — `rule:classes/no-dynamic-properties` makes the declared list exhaustive for every class kind
--FILE--
<?nvs
try {
    throw new LogicError("x");
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    // `message` is declared, so this resolves like any other property —
    // the exception tree's own members are in the signature table.
    echo $e->message, "\n";
    // A name that tree does not declare was excused from `E0405` by the
    // *class kind* rather than by the name, which left the access with no
    // typed-expression entry and nothing in front of `nvs-ir`'s panic.
    echo $e->nope, "\n";
    // The write half asks the same question and now gets the same answer.
    $e->nope = "y";
}
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0405]: `Throwable` has no property named `nope`
   --> %s:11:10
   |
11 |     echo $e->nope, "\n";
   |          ^^ referenced here

error[E0405]: `Throwable` has no property named `nope`
   --> %s:13:5
   |
13 |     $e->nope = "y";
   |     ^^ referenced here

error: aborting due to 2 errors
